The Flatpak backend builds AppStream source ids with a "<name> (system)"
suffix for system remotes so that user and system copies of the same
remote stay distinct in the store's caches (ref_to_package matches on
the suffixed id). The constructor, however, passed that suffixed id back
into update_appstream_sync(), which libflatpak treats as a *remote name*
(the C function documents its argument as "@remote_name: the name of the
remote", flatpak-installation.c) and errors with
`Remote "<name> (system)" not found` (raised in flatpak_dir_update_appstream,
flatpak-dir.c).

Because a cold start loads caches with refresh=false, the suffixed-id
call is the Store's only self-healing attempt, so it can never fetch the
AppStream catalog for system remotes (flathub on Void, fedora /
fedora-testing on Fedora). The Browse grid stays empty until the catalog
is produced elsewhere -- the store's post-update refresh, which uses the
plain name, or `flatpak update --appstream` run as root.

Use the plain remote name for the fetch only; the suffixed id remains
the cache identity key. The plain name has to be bound at loop scope --
previously it lived only inside the `remote.name()` match arm -- so the
patch hoists it and derives the cache `source_id` from it.

Confirmed still needed against epoch-1.10.0 upstream (tag epoch-1.10.0,
src/backend/flatpak.rs:83). On the next epoch bump, verify whether
upstream has repaired this update call (or dropped the "(system)"
suffix) before dropping this patch -- see docs/UPSTREAM.md
"Carried patches".

